<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chesapeake Bay</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chesapeake Bay is the largest estuary in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, extending about 300 kilometres from the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susquehanna-river">Susquehanna River</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a> to the Atlantic in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>. Its shores were home to Algonquian peoples such as the Powhatan and to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susquehannock">Susquehannock</a>, and the English colonies of Virginia and Maryland grew along its rivers from 1607 on tobacco exports.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chesapeake-bay.jpg" alt="Chesapeake Bay" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Landsat/NASA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chesapeakelandsat.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Chesapeake Bay lies in the Mid-Atlantic region, separated from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a> mainly by the Delmarva Peninsula. Its mouth is between Cape Henry and Cape Charles. More than 150 major rivers and streams flow through its drainage basin, which covers parts of six states and all of Washington, D.C. The bay is 2.8 miles (4.5 km) wide at its narrowest and 30 miles (48 km) at its widest, and its average depth is 21 feet (6.4 m). It is the drowned valley of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susquehanna-river">Susquehanna River</a>, where the river once flowed when sea level was lower. The name comes from an Algonquian word, Chesepiooc, referring to a village at a big river.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Paleoindians may have lived around the bay 11,000 years ago. For thousands of years Native American societies lived in villages of wooden longhouses near the water, where they fished and farmed beans, corn, tobacco and squash. Later, communities formed confederations such as the Powhatan, the Piscataway and the Nanticoke, each led by a central chief.</p><p>Giovanni da Verrazzano sailed past the bay in 1524 in the service of the French crown. Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> founded the short-lived Ajacan Mission on a Chesapeake tributary in 1570. In 1781 the bay was the site of the Battle of the Chesapeake, or Battle of the Capes, in which a French fleet defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> in the decisive naval battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a>. The victory allowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a> and the Comte de Rochambeau to trap <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-cornwallis-1st-marquess-cornwallis">Lord Cornwallis</a>'s army at Yorktown, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The bay forms a link in the Intracoastal Waterway, joined to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delaware-river">Delaware River</a> by the Chesapeake and Delaware Canal. A shipping channel, dredged by the US Army Corps of Engineers since the 1850s, serves the Port of Baltimore. Two bridges span the bay, the Chesapeake Bay Bridge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a> and the Chesapeake Bay Bridge-Tunnel in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>. It is known for blue crabs, clams and oysters. In the 1970s it was found to contain one of the first identified marine dead zones, and a report of 1983 called it an ecosystem in decline. More than 1,800 wrecks of ships and boats lie on its bed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chesapeake Bay” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>According to the English writer William Strachey he killed the chief of the Kecoughtan around 1597 and installed his young son Pochins in his place, and before 1607 he destroyed the Chesepians near the mouth of the <b>Chesapeake Bay</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/powhatan-confederacy">Powhatan Confederacy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>With the sanction of President Madison, Key went to Baltimore in early September 1814 and, with John Stuart Skinner, an American agent for prisoners of war, sailed down the <b>Chesapeake Bay</b> to the British fleet.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-star-spangled-banner">The Star-Spangled Banner</a></li><li><blockquote>When John Smith met them in 1608 on <b>Chesapeake Bay</b> he described them as tall and well armed.</blockquote><a href="/state/susquehannock">Susquehannock</a></li></ul></section>
